from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Around 1900, classical physics could not explain several experiments. Which problem below helped push physicists toward new theories?","options":["the orbits of the planets","the glow of hot objects and the photoelectric effect","the fall of an apple from a tree","the balancing of a lever"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Blackbody radiation and the photoelectric effect resisted every classical explanation and launched quantum theory."},{"prompt":"Special relativity assumes the speed of light in a vacuum is...","options":["different for every observer","the same for all observers, whatever their motion","infinite","constant only here on Earth"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The constancy of the light speed for all observers is one of the two founding postulates."},{"prompt":"The equation E=mc^2 says that...","options":["energy and mass are unrelated","mass is a concentrated form of energy","light carries no energy","only moving objects have mass"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Mass and energy are two forms of the same thing, linked by the large factor c squared."},{"prompt":"In general relativity, gravity is best described as...","options":["a force carried by magnets","the curving of spacetime by mass and energy","friction between planets","a kind of sound wave"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Mass and energy curve spacetime, and free objects follow the straightest available paths through that curved geometry."},{"prompt":"In the photoelectric effect, light ejects electrons from a metal only when...","options":["the light is bright enough","the light frequency is above a threshold","the metal is hot","the light is red"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each photon carries energy set by its frequency; below a threshold frequency no electrons escape, however bright the beam."},{"prompt":"Wave-particle duality means that...","options":["waves and particles never mix","light and matter each show both wave and particle behavior","only sound behaves as a wave","electrons are always pure particles"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Light and matter display wave-like or particle-like behavior depending on the experiment you run."},{"prompt":"The Heisenberg uncertainty principle states that...","options":["every measurement is simply wrong","position and momentum cannot both be known with unlimited precision","energy is never conserved","nothing can ever be measured"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The more sharply you pin down position, the less sharply momentum is defined, and the reverse holds too."},{"prompt":"Nuclear fusion releases energy by...","options":["splitting heavy nuclei apart","joining light nuclei into heavier ones","cooling atoms until they stop","slowing down electrons"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fusion merges light nuclei such as hydrogen into heavier ones and releases energy; fission instead splits heavy nuclei."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'Why classical physics broke down around 1900', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain what physicists mean by classical physics, and name the experiments around 1900 that it simply could not account for. By the end of this lesson you will be able to say, in plain words, why the glow of hot objects, the photoelectric effect, and the stability of atoms each pointed past the older theories toward something deeper. You will also see why the search for the ether, the supposed medium that carried light waves, ended in a famous null result. Warm-up: before you read on, think of one everyday thing that changes color as it heats up, such as a stove element going from dull red to bright orange. Classical physics predicted that such a glowing object should pour out an impossible flood of energy at short wavelengths. Keep that puzzle in mind, because fixing it is where quantum physics begins. These failures were not small corrections. They were places where the best theories of the day gave answers that were not just slightly off but wildly, absurdly wrong, which is exactly the kind of crack that forces a scientific revolution."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"By the late 1800s physics looked almost finished. Newton''s laws described motion and gravity, Maxwell''s equations unified electricity, magnetism, and light, and thermodynamics explained heat. Yet four cracks spread through this confident picture. First, blackbody radiation: the math for how a hot object should glow predicted infinite energy at short wavelengths, a nonsense result nicknamed the ultraviolet catastrophe. Second, the photoelectric effect: light shining on a metal knocked out electrons in a way that depended on the light frequency, not its brightness, which wave theory could not explain. Third, atomic spectra and atomic stability: atoms emitted light only at sharp, specific colors, and classical theory said the orbiting electrons should spiral into the nucleus in a fraction of a second. Fourth, the Michelson-Morley experiment found no sign of the ether, the medium light was supposed to travel through, hinting that the speed of light was strangely absolute. Each crack demanded a new idea. Relativity answered the questions about space, time, and light speed, while quantum theory answered the questions about radiation, atoms, and the photoelectric effect."},{"heading":"Key ideas & examples","text":"Consider a piece of iron in a forge. As it heats, it glows red, then orange, then white. Classical theory, using what is called the Rayleigh-Jeans law, matched the glow at long wavelengths but blew up to infinity at short ones, so it predicted that every warm object would blast out deadly short-wave radiation. Nothing of the sort happens. In 1900 Max Planck fixed the formula by assuming energy comes in discrete lumps, or quanta, of size E = h times f, where h is Planck''s constant and f is the frequency. For the photoelectric effect, shining dim blue light could free electrons while bright red light could not, which makes no sense if light is just a continuous wave whose energy grows with brightness. For atomic spectra, hydrogen gas glows only at a fixed set of colors, like a barcode, not a smooth rainbow. And the Michelson-Morley experiment, which split a light beam and recombined it, found the same light speed no matter how the apparatus moved. Four clean, repeatable results, and classical physics failed all four. That is the setup for everything that follows."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name the three pillars of classical physics. 2) What absurd prediction did classical theory make about the short-wavelength glow of hot objects? 3) In the photoelectric effect, which property of the light decides whether electrons are freed - brightness or frequency? 4) What did the Michelson-Morley experiment fail to find? 5) Who introduced the idea that energy comes in discrete quanta, and in what year? 6) Why does classical theory wrongly predict that atoms should collapse? 7) Which two broad theories grew out of these four failures? Answers: 1) Newton''s mechanics, Maxwell''s electromagnetism, and thermodynamics. 2) That it should emit infinite energy at short wavelengths - the ultraviolet catastrophe. 3) The frequency; below a threshold frequency no electrons escape, however bright the light. 4) Any sign of the ether, the medium light was supposed to travel through, so the light speed looked absolute. 5) Max Planck, in 1900, with the relation E = h times f. 6) Because an orbiting, accelerating electron should continuously radiate energy and spiral into the nucleus in a fraction of a second. 7) Relativity, for space, time, and light speed, and quantum theory, for radiation and atoms."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking classical physics was simply wrong and useless. It is extremely accurate for everyday speeds and sizes, and engineers and architects still rely on it every day; it only breaks down at very high speeds and at the atomic scale, where the new theories take over. Mistake: believing brighter light always carries more energy per particle. Brightness means more photons arriving, but each individual photon''s energy is set by frequency alone, not by how many of them there are. Mistake: assuming atoms are tiny solar systems that are perfectly stable for no reason; classical theory actually predicts they should collapse almost instantly as the orbiting electrons radiate their energy away. Mistake: treating the four puzzles as minor rounding errors; each was a clean, repeatable result that classical physics got wildly wrong. Exit: (a) What is the ultraviolet catastrophe, in one sentence? (b) What two broad theories grew out of these failures? Answers: the false classical prediction that a hot object radiates infinite energy at short wavelengths; relativity and quantum theory."}]}'::jsonb, 'Classical physics means the pre-1900 framework of Newton''s mechanics, Maxwell''s electromagnetism, and thermodynamics - very powerful, but nearly silent about the atomic scale and about speeds close to that of light.', 12),
  (3, 'Special relativity: light, time, and E=mc^2', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"State the two postulates of special relativity and use them to explain time dilation, length contraction, and the equivalence of mass and energy captured by E=mc^2. By the end you will be able to say why two observers moving relative to each other can honestly disagree about how long a clock ticks or how long a ruler is, and yet both be right. You will also understand why nothing with mass can reach the speed of light. Warm-up: imagine you are on a train moving at a steady speed with the blinds down, so you feel no bumps. Is there any experiment you could do inside the sealed car to tell whether you are moving or standing still? Einstein''s answer is no, and that simple idea, taken seriously, reshapes space and time. Hold onto the train image, because we will return to it. The surprises of relativity are not illusions or measurement errors; they are real features of a universe where the speed of light is the same for everyone."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"In 1905 Einstein built special relativity on two postulates. First, the laws of physics are the same for all observers moving at constant velocity, so there is no special resting frame. Second, the speed of light in a vacuum, c, is the same for every observer, no matter how the source or the observer moves. These sound modest, but together they force space and time to bend. If light speed is fixed for everyone, then measurements of time and distance must flex to keep it so. Time dilation means a moving clock ticks slower as seen by a stationary observer. Length contraction means a moving object is measured as shorter along its direction of motion. Both effects grow with the Lorentz factor gamma = 1 / sqrt(1 - v^2/c^2), which is near 1 at ordinary speeds and shoots up as v nears c. Finally, the theory ties mass and energy together: a body at rest already holds energy E=mc^2, and adding kinetic energy makes it harder and harder to accelerate, so no massive object can ever be pushed all the way to light speed."},{"heading":"Key ideas & examples","text":"Time dilation is not a thought experiment alone; it is measured daily. Muons created high in the atmosphere live only about two microseconds at rest, yet they reach the ground because, moving near c, their internal clocks run slow from our point of view. The global positioning satellites carry atomic clocks that must be corrected for relativity, or navigation would drift by kilometers per day. For length contraction, a spaceship passing at a large fraction of c would be measured as squashed along its travel direction. For E=mc^2, the numbers are striking: because c^2 is about nine times ten to the sixteenth in units of meters squared per second squared, even a gram of mass holds the energy of a large bomb. The Sun shines by converting about four million tonnes of mass into energy every second through fusion. A useful sanity check: plug v = 0 into gamma and you get 1, so a resting object sees no time dilation or contraction, and relativity smoothly reduces to familiar Newtonian physics at low speed."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) State the two postulates of special relativity. 2) Does a moving clock, seen from rest, tick faster or slower? 3) Along which direction is a fast-moving ruler contracted? 4) What is the value of the Lorentz factor gamma when v = 0? 5) In words, what does E=mc^2 claim about mass? 6) Why can no object with mass reach the speed of light? Answers: 1) The laws of physics are the same for all constant-velocity observers, and light speed in a vacuum is the same for all of them. 2) Slower. 3) Along its direction of motion. 4) Exactly 1, so there is no dilation or contraction at rest. 5) Mass is a concentrated store of energy; the two are interchangeable. 6) As speed rises, the energy needed to accelerate it grows without limit, approaching infinity at c."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking time dilation is just clocks malfunctioning; it is a real difference in elapsed time, confirmed over and over by fast particles and by the atomic clocks on navigation satellites. Mistake: believing one observer is really moving and the other really at rest; at constant velocity each one sees the other''s clock run slow, and both descriptions are equally correct, because there is no privileged resting frame. Mistake: reading E=mc^2 as saying mass turns into energy only in bombs; even a perfectly stationary object already holds a rest energy of mc^2, and the equation works in both directions. Mistake: imagining you could just keep pushing something past light speed; as speed approaches c the energy required grows without bound, so the light speed is an unreachable ceiling for anything with mass. Mistake: confusing the Lorentz factor at rest; at v = 0 it equals 1, so slow everyday motion shows no measurable relativity. Exit: (a) What stays the same for every observer, and is the key postulate behind all these effects? (b) Write the rest energy of an object of mass m in symbols. Answers: the speed of light in a vacuum, c; E=mc^2."}]}'::jsonb, 'The Lorentz factor, often written gamma, grows as speed grows: gamma = 1 / sqrt(1 - v^2/c^2). At everyday speeds v is tiny next to c, so gamma is almost exactly 1 and relativity effects vanish from view.', 14),
  (4, 'General relativity: gravity as curved spacetime', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain, at a conceptual level, how general relativity replaces the idea of gravity as a pulling force with the idea of gravity as the curving of spacetime by mass and energy. By the end you will be able to describe the equivalence principle, say what it means for a planet to follow a straight path through curved space, and name a few predictions that have been confirmed, such as the bending of starlight and the slowing of clocks in strong gravity. Warm-up: picture dropping a ball inside a sealed elevator. If the elevator is sitting on the ground, the ball falls to the floor. If instead the elevator is far out in space and being pulled upward at a steady acceleration, the ball again moves to the floor in exactly the same way. Could any experiment inside the box tell gravity from acceleration? Einstein argued no, and that insight, the equivalence principle, is the seed of the whole theory. This lesson stays conceptual and uses no heavy mathematics."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Special relativity handled constant-velocity motion but left gravity untouched. In 1915 Einstein extended the picture with general relativity. The central claim is that gravity is not a force reaching across empty space, as Newton imagined, but the shape of spacetime itself. Mass and energy tell spacetime how to curve, and that curvature tells matter how to move. A useful image is a heavy ball resting on a stretched rubber sheet: it makes a dip, and a marble rolling nearby curves toward it, not because a rope pulls it but because the surface is bent. The planets orbit the Sun because they follow geodesics, the straightest available paths through the spacetime the Sun has curved. The equivalence principle underpins all of this: being at rest in a gravitational field feels exactly like being accelerated in empty space, so gravity and acceleration are locally the same thing. Because this geometry governs even light and time, clocks run slower where gravity is stronger, and light paths bend as they pass massive bodies."},{"heading":"Key ideas & examples","text":"General relativity has passed many tests. In 1919 astronomers measured starlight bending as it grazed the Sun during an eclipse, shifting the apparent positions of stars by just the predicted amount. The orbit of Mercury drifts, or precesses, by a tiny extra amount each century that Newton''s theory could not explain but general relativity nails exactly. Clocks tick slightly faster on a mountaintop than at sea level because gravity is weaker higher up, an effect called gravitational time dilation that the positioning satellites must correct for. The theory also predicts black holes, regions where spacetime is curved so steeply that not even light escapes, and gravitational waves, ripples in spacetime set off by violent events such as colliding black holes. Those waves were detected directly in 2015, a full century after the theory predicted them, when instruments sensed the faint stretching and squeezing of space from a merger more than a billion light years away. Each confirmation shows that treating gravity as geometry is not a metaphor but a precise, working description of nature."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In one sentence, what causes gravity according to general relativity? 2) What is the equivalence principle? 3) What is a geodesic, in plain words? 4) Name one prediction of general relativity confirmed by observing starlight. 5) Do clocks run faster or slower where gravity is stronger? 6) What violent cosmic events were detected in 2015 that the theory had predicted a century earlier? 7) What is a black hole, in the language of this lesson? 8) Why does the orbit of Mercury support general relativity over Newton''s theory? Answers: 1) Mass and energy curve spacetime, and that curvature is what we feel as gravity. 2) Standing in a gravitational field is locally indistinguishable from being accelerated in empty space. 3) The straightest possible path through a curved space. 4) The bending of starlight passing near the Sun, seen during the 1919 eclipse. 5) Slower where gravity is stronger. 6) Gravitational waves, ripples in spacetime from merging black holes. 7) A region where spacetime is curved so steeply that not even light can escape. 8) Its orbit precesses by a small extra amount each century that Newton''s theory cannot explain but general relativity predicts exactly."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: picturing gravity as an invisible rope that pulls objects together; general relativity replaces that pulling force entirely with curved geometry, so there is nothing reaching across the gap. Mistake: thinking the rubber-sheet image is literally true; it is only a two-dimensional cartoon meant to hint at curvature that really happens in four-dimensional spacetime. Mistake: believing that orbiting objects are being actively steered or held; they coast freely along geodesics and feel no force at all while in free fall, which is why astronauts float. Mistake: assuming time runs at one universal rate everywhere; it depends on where you sit in a gravitational field, running slower deep in a strong field. Mistake: treating the theory as untested speculation; it has passed every precise test from eclipse starlight to gravitational waves. Exit: (a) Fill in the slogan: mass tells spacetime how to curve, and curved spacetime tells matter how to ____. (b) Name one everyday technology that must account for gravitational time dilation. Answers: move; the satellite positioning system used for navigation."}]}'::jsonb, 'A geodesic is the straightest possible path through a curved space - the four-dimensional version of a great-circle route on the surface of the Earth. In general relativity, free-falling objects follow geodesics.', 13),
  (5, 'The quantum revolution: blackbody radiation and the photoelectric effect', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain how two stubborn puzzles - the glow of hot objects and the photoelectric effect - forced physicists to accept that energy and light come in discrete packets called quanta. By the end you will be able to state Planck''s relation E = h times f in words, describe why the photoelectric effect depends on frequency rather than brightness, and connect both to the idea that light is made of photons. Warm-up: suppose you shine a very bright red lamp on a metal plate and no electrons come off, then switch to a dim violet lamp and electrons immediately appear. How can the dimmer light do what the brighter light could not? If you treat light as a smooth continuous wave, this is baffling: more brightness should mean more energy. The resolution, that light arrives in lumps whose individual energy depends on color, is the heart of the quantum revolution and the reason the classical picture had to give way."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The quantum story opens in 1900 with Max Planck. To fix the ultraviolet catastrophe, he assumed that a glowing object can only emit or absorb energy in discrete chunks of size E = h times f, where h is Planck''s constant, a very small fixed number, and f is the frequency of the light. This single assumption cured the runaway prediction and matched the observed glow perfectly, though Planck at first saw it as a mathematical trick. In 1905 Einstein took the quanta seriously as real particles of light, later named photons, and used them to explain the photoelectric effect. Each photon carries energy set only by its frequency. A photon must deliver at least a minimum amount of energy, the work function, to knock an electron free from a metal. Below a threshold frequency, no single photon has enough energy, so no electrons escape however many photons you pour on. Above threshold, raising the frequency raises each electron''s energy, while raising the brightness only increases how many electrons come out. Energy, it turned out, is grainy."},{"heading":"Key ideas & examples","text":"Think of photons as coins and the metal as a vending machine that needs at least one coin of a certain value. A flood of low-value coins, the dim red photons, never adds up to free an electron, because the machine only takes one coin at a time and each is too small. A single high-value coin, a violet photon, works instantly. This is exactly the puzzle from the warm-up, now solved. Planck''s constant h is about six point six times ten to the minus thirty-four joule-seconds, so tiny that the graininess of energy is invisible in everyday life but dominant inside atoms. The photoelectric effect is not a curiosity: it drives solar cells, which turn photons into electric current, and the light sensors in cameras and automatic doors. Einstein received the Nobel Prize chiefly for explaining it, not for relativity. And Planck''s relation E = h times f is one of the most used equations in all of physics, linking a photon''s color directly to its energy: bluer light means higher frequency means more energy per photon."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Write Planck''s relation between a photon''s energy and its frequency, in symbols. 2) In the photoelectric effect, does brightness or frequency decide whether any electrons escape? 3) What is the work function of a metal? 4) Why does dim violet light free electrons when bright red light cannot? 5) Name one everyday device that relies on the photoelectric effect. 6) Who first proposed energy quanta, and who explained the photoelectric effect with photons? Answers: 1) E = h times f. 2) Frequency - below a threshold frequency, no electrons come off at all. 3) The minimum energy needed to pull an electron free from the metal surface. 4) Each violet photon carries enough energy on its own, while each red photon falls short no matter how many arrive. 5) Solar cells, camera light sensors, or automatic-door detectors. 6) Max Planck proposed quanta; Einstein explained the photoelectric effect with photons."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking a brighter beam always gives each electron more energy; brightness adds more photons, but each electron is freed by a single photon whose energy depends only on frequency, so a brighter beam just frees more electrons, not more energetic ones. Mistake: believing Planck''s constant is large; it is extraordinarily small, about six point six times ten to the minus thirty-four, which is exactly why quantum graininess stays hidden at human scales. Mistake: treating photons as little billiard balls in every situation; they also behave as spreading waves, a double nature the next lesson explores in full. Mistake: confusing frequency and brightness - frequency is the color of the light, while brightness is simply the number of photons arriving each second. Mistake: assuming Planck immediately believed in real light particles; he first saw quanta as a mathematical fix, and it was Einstein who took them seriously as photons. Exit: (a) What quantity, besides Planck''s constant, sets a photon''s energy? (b) Below the threshold frequency, how many electrons are freed, no matter how bright the light? Answers: the frequency of the light; none."}]}'::jsonb, 'A quantum is a smallest indivisible packet of something - here, of energy. The plural is quanta. The photon is the quantum of light, carrying energy E = h times f, where f is the frequency.', 14),
  (6, 'Wave-particle duality, the photon, and the Bohr model', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain wave-particle duality - the idea that both light and matter show wave-like and particle-like behavior - and describe how the Bohr model used quantized orbits to explain the sharp colors atoms emit. By the end you will be able to say what the double-slit experiment reveals, write the de Broglie relation lambda = h / p in words, and connect electron energy jumps to the barcode-like spectral lines of hydrogen. Warm-up: light clearly behaves as a wave when it spreads and makes interference fringes, yet it also behaves as a stream of photons in the photoelectric effect. Could matter, such as electrons, do the same trick in reverse - behave as a wave even though we think of it as particles? The answer, confirmed by experiment, is yes, and it forces us to abandon the comfortable idea that everything is either a wave or a particle. In the quantum world, things are both, and which face they show depends on the question you ask."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Light shows two faces. It spreads and interferes like a wave, yet it delivers energy in photon lumps like a particle. In 1924 Louis de Broglie proposed that matter is the same: every particle has a wavelength lambda = h / p, where p is its momentum. Electrons fired one at a time through a double slit slowly build up an interference pattern, proving they travel as waves even though each lands as a single dot like a particle. This duality is universal, but the wavelengths of large objects are so tiny that only atomic-scale things reveal it. Meanwhile, in 1913 Niels Bohr applied quantum ideas to the atom. He proposed that electrons can only occupy certain allowed orbits, each with a fixed energy, and that they neither radiate nor spiral inward while in an orbit. An electron emits or absorbs light only when it jumps between orbits, and the photon''s energy equals the difference between the two orbit energies. Because the allowed energies are fixed, the emitted colors are fixed too, which explains why each element has its own sharp spectral fingerprint."},{"heading":"Key ideas & examples","text":"The double-slit experiment is the cleanest demonstration of duality. Send photons or electrons through two narrow slits and a striped interference pattern appears, the signature of waves overlapping. Yet detectors always register whole particles, one at a time, never half a photon. Try to watch which slit each particle goes through, and the stripes vanish - measurement changes the outcome. For the Bohr model, hydrogen is the showcase. Its electron can sit only in orbits labeled n = 1, 2, 3, and so on, with energies that get closer together as n grows. When an electron falls from a higher orbit to a lower one, it releases a photon whose color matches the energy gap, producing the familiar red, blue-green, and violet lines of the hydrogen spectrum. This is why street lamps glow with element-specific colors and why astronomers can read the composition of distant stars from their light. The de Broglie idea even explains Bohr''s orbits: the allowed orbits are exactly those where a whole number of electron wavelengths fits around the circle."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Write the de Broglie relation for a particle''s wavelength in symbols. 2) What pattern appears when electrons pass one at a time through a double slit? 3) In the Bohr model, when does an atom emit a photon? 4) Why does each element produce its own set of spectral lines? 5) Why do we never see the wave nature of a thrown baseball? 6) What happens to the interference pattern if you measure which slit each particle takes? Answers: 1) lambda = h / p. 2) A wave interference pattern of stripes, built up dot by dot. 3) When an electron jumps from a higher-energy orbit to a lower-energy one. 4) Because its allowed electron energies, and so the energy gaps, are unique to that element. 5) Its momentum is huge, so its de Broglie wavelength is far too small to notice. 6) It disappears; the particles then behave as if there were no interference."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking light is really a wave and the particle behavior is an illusion, or the reverse; both faces are genuine, and which one appears depends on the experiment you choose to run. Mistake: imagining electrons smear out like permanent clouds; each still arrives as one whole particle at a single spot, yet the places it is likely to land follow a wave pattern built up over many particles. Mistake: treating Bohr''s orbits as tiny planetary paths; they are allowed energy states, and the model is a useful stepping stone toward full quantum mechanics, not the final word. Mistake: expecting continuous rainbows from atoms; isolated atoms emit only sharp lines at fixed colors, a barcode unique to each element. Mistake: assuming duality matters for big objects; their de Broglie wavelength is so tiny that the wave side is utterly invisible. Exit: (a) What single experiment best shows that electrons behave as waves? (b) In an atom, the energy of an emitted photon equals the difference between what two things? Answers: the double-slit experiment; the two orbit, or energy-level, energies the electron jumps between."}]}'::jsonb, 'The de Broglie wavelength of a particle is lambda = h / p, where p is its momentum. Big, fast, heavy objects have tiny wavelengths, which is why we never notice the wave nature of a thrown baseball.', 14),
  (7, 'The uncertainty principle, the wavefunction, and quantum tunneling', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain, conceptually, the Heisenberg uncertainty principle, the meaning of the wavefunction as a probability wave, and quantum tunneling, the ability of particles to cross barriers they classically could not. By the end you will be able to say why position and momentum cannot both be known perfectly, why quantum mechanics predicts probabilities rather than exact paths, and how tunneling powers real devices and even the Sun. Warm-up: roll a ball toward a hill it does not have enough energy to climb, and classically it always rolls back - every time, without exception. Now imagine a tiny particle meeting a similar energy barrier. Quantum mechanics says there is a small but real chance the particle simply appears on the far side, as if it passed through the hill. This is not a trick or a measurement error; it is a genuine feature of how matter behaves at small scales, and whole technologies depend on it. Keep the ball-and-hill image in mind as we build the ideas up."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"At the heart of quantum mechanics is the wavefunction, usually written psi. It is not the particle itself but a wave of probability: the square of its size at a point tells you the odds of finding the particle there if you look. Before measurement, a particle does not have one definite position; it is spread across possibilities, and measuring forces a single outcome. The Heisenberg uncertainty principle follows from this wave nature. It says you cannot simultaneously know both a particle''s position and its momentum with unlimited precision; sharpening one blurs the other. This is not a flaw in our instruments but a built-in feature of nature. Quantum tunneling is another consequence. Because the wavefunction does not stop abruptly at a barrier but tapers off inside and beyond it, there is a nonzero probability that a particle is found on the far side of an energy barrier it could never cross by classical rules. The thinner and lower the barrier, the higher the chance of tunneling through."},{"heading":"Key ideas & examples","text":"The uncertainty principle has real teeth. It explains why electrons do not crash into the nucleus: confining an electron to a tiny region forces a large spread in momentum, and that motion keeps it from collapsing inward. It also means empty space is never truly still, since energy and time obey their own uncertainty relation. The wavefunction picture is confirmed every time an interference pattern builds up from single particles landing where the probability is high and avoiding where it is low. Quantum tunneling is everywhere in technology. In the scanning tunneling microscope, electrons tunnel across the gap between a sharp tip and a surface, letting us image individual atoms. In flash memory, electrons tunnel through thin insulating layers to store data. Alpha decay, a form of radioactivity, happens when particles tunnel out of a nucleus. Most important of all, the Sun shines because protons tunnel through their mutual electric repulsion to fuse; without tunneling, the core would not be hot enough for fusion, and the stars would not burn."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In words, what does the square of the wavefunction tell you? 2) State the uncertainty principle in one sentence. 3) Is the uncertainty principle a limit of our instruments or a feature of nature? 4) What is quantum tunneling? 5) Name two real devices or processes that rely on tunneling. 6) Why, roughly, does confining an electron tightly give it large momentum spread? Answers: 1) The probability of finding the particle at that location when you measure. 2) You cannot know a particle''s position and its momentum both with unlimited precision at the same time. 3) A fundamental feature of nature, not merely an instrument limit. 4) A particle crossing an energy barrier it could not cross under classical rules, because its probability wave extends beyond the barrier. 5) The scanning tunneling microscope, flash memory, alpha decay, or fusion in the Sun. 6) Squeezing its position into a small region forces the spread in its momentum to grow, by the uncertainty relation."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reading the uncertainty principle as ''everything is unknowable''; it is a precise trade-off between specific pairs of quantities, not a blanket of ignorance. Mistake: thinking the wavefunction is a physical substance you could touch; it is a mathematical object whose square gives probabilities. Mistake: imagining tunneling lets large everyday objects pass through walls; the probability shrinks so fast with size and barrier width that it is utterly negligible for anything bigger than atoms. Mistake: believing a measured particle was always at the spot you found it; before the measurement it had no single definite position. Exit: (a) Which two quantities trade off in the Heisenberg uncertainty principle? (b) What lets a particle cross a barrier it classically could not? Answers: position and momentum; quantum tunneling, because the probability wave extends through and past the barrier."}]}'::jsonb, 'The wavefunction, often written with the Greek letter psi, does not give a particle''s exact location. Its square gives the probability of finding the particle at each place - quantum mechanics predicts odds, not certainties.', 14),
  (8, 'The standard model and nuclear physics in brief', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Give a brief, organized tour of the standard model of particle physics and of nuclear physics. By the end you will be able to name the main families of fundamental particles, say what holds a nucleus together, and distinguish the three kinds of radioactivity as well as nuclear fission and fusion. Warm-up: everything you can see is built from atoms, and atoms from a nucleus surrounded by electrons. But is the nucleus itself made of smaller pieces, and are those pieces truly fundamental or made of something smaller still? Modern physics answers that protons and neutrons are not fundamental - they are built from quarks - while electrons appear to have no inner parts at all. This lesson gathers the smallest known building blocks and the forces between them into a single organized picture, then turns to the nucleus to see how its energy is locked up and released. We will keep the mathematics light and focus on the structure and the vocabulary."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The standard model is our best catalog of the fundamental particles and three of the four forces. Matter is built from two families: quarks, which combine to form protons and neutrons, and leptons, which include the electron and the elusive neutrino. Forces are carried by exchange particles: the photon carries electromagnetism, gluons carry the strong force that binds quarks, and the W and Z bosons carry the weak force behind some radioactivity. The Higgs boson, confirmed in 2012, is tied to how particles acquire mass. Gravity is the one force the standard model does not yet include. Nuclear physics zooms in on the nucleus, a tight cluster of protons and neutrons held together by the strong force against the electric repulsion of the protons. Some nuclei are unstable and release energy through radioactivity. Rearranging nuclei can liberate enormous energy: splitting a heavy nucleus is fission, and merging light nuclei is fusion. Both convert a little mass into energy according to E=mc^2, which is why nuclear processes dwarf chemical ones."},{"heading":"Key ideas & examples","text":"A proton is made of three quarks, two up and one down; a neutron is two down and one up. The strong force, carried by gluons, is so powerful at short range that it overcomes the fierce electric repulsion crammed protons feel. Radioactivity comes in three classic types: alpha decay emits a helium nucleus, beta decay emits an electron as a neutron turns into a proton, and gamma decay emits a high-energy photon. Carbon-14, used in radiocarbon dating, undergoes beta decay with a half-life of about 5,700 years. Fission is the energy source of nuclear reactors and early atomic weapons: a neutron splits a uranium-235 nucleus into lighter fragments plus more neutrons, which can sustain a chain reaction. Fusion is the opposite and powers the Sun and the stars, joining hydrogen nuclei into helium and releasing far more energy per kilogram than fission. Building a controlled fusion reactor on Earth is one of the great engineering goals of the century, because its fuel is abundant and it leaves little long-lived waste."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name the two families of matter particles in the standard model. 2) Which particle carries the electromagnetic force? 3) What force holds the nucleus together against proton repulsion? 4) Name the three classic types of radioactive decay. 5) What is the difference between fission and fusion? 6) What is a proton made of? 7) What does the mass number of a nucleus count? 8) Which force is responsible for beta decay, and which particle carries it? Answers: 1) Quarks and leptons; quarks build protons and neutrons, while leptons include the electron and the neutrino. 2) The photon. 3) The strong force, carried by gluons, which overpowers the electric repulsion of the packed protons. 4) Alpha decay, beta decay, and gamma decay. 5) Fission splits a heavy nucleus into lighter fragments, while fusion joins light nuclei into a heavier one; both can release energy by converting a little mass according to E=mc^2. 6) Three quarks - two up quarks and one down quark. 7) The total number of protons plus neutrons in the nucleus. 8) The weak force, carried by the W and Z bosons."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking protons and neutrons are fundamental; they are composite, built from quarks bound by gluons, while the electron so far shows no inner structure at all. Mistake: believing the standard model explains everything; it leaves out gravity and does not account for dark matter or dark energy, so it is powerful but incomplete. Mistake: confusing fission and fusion; fission splits heavy nuclei such as uranium, fusion merges light nuclei such as hydrogen, and both can release energy per kilogram far beyond any chemical reaction. Mistake: assuming all radioactivity is the same; alpha, beta, and gamma decay emit different things - a helium nucleus, an electron, and a high-energy photon - and they penetrate matter to very different depths, from stopped by paper to needing thick lead. Exit: (a) Which fundamental force is missing from the standard model? (b) Which process powers the Sun, fission or fusion? (c) What is a neutron made of? Answers: gravity; fusion; two down quarks and one up quark."}]}'::jsonb, 'A nucleus is written as a chemical symbol with its mass number, as in carbon-14 or uranium-235. The mass number counts protons plus neutrons; the number of protons alone fixes which element it is.', 13),
  (9, 'Key terms', 'vocab', '{"items":[{"term":"quantum","translation":"the smallest indivisible packet of a physical quantity, such as a packet of energy","example":"Planck proposed that a hot object emits energy only in whole quanta of size E = h times f."},{"term":"photon","translation":"the quantum, or particle, of light, carrying energy set by its frequency","example":"A single violet photon has more energy than a single red photon."},{"term":"time dilation","translation":"the slowing of a moving clock as measured by a stationary observer","example":"Fast-moving muons reach the ground because time dilation stretches their short lifetimes."},{"term":"length contraction","translation":"the shortening of a fast-moving object along its direction of motion, as measured by an observer at rest","example":"A spaceship passing near light speed is measured as squashed by length contraction."},{"term":"spacetime","translation":"the unified four-dimensional stage of three space dimensions and one time dimension that gravity curves","example":"In general relativity, mass bends spacetime, and planets follow the straightest paths through it."},{"term":"wave-particle duality","translation":"the fact that light and matter each show both wave-like and particle-like behavior","example":"Electrons build an interference pattern in the double-slit experiment, showing wave-particle duality."},{"term":"wavefunction","translation":"the mathematical object, often written psi, whose square gives the probability of finding a particle at each location","example":"The wavefunction does not fix where the electron is, only the odds of finding it there."},{"term":"quantum tunneling","translation":"a particle crossing an energy barrier it could not cross under classical rules, because its probability wave extends beyond it","example":"The Sun shines because protons tunnel through their electric repulsion to fuse."},{"term":"half-life","translation":"the time for half of a sample of a radioactive substance to decay","example":"Carbon-14 has a half-life of about 5,700 years, which makes it useful for dating."}]}'::jsonb, null, 9),
  (10, 'Checkpoint: relativity and the quantum world', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Max Planck solved the ultraviolet catastrophe by assuming that energy is...","options":["emitted continuously","emitted only in discrete quanta of size E = h times f","never emitted at all","emitted only as sound"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Treating energy as discrete quanta removed the false infinite prediction and matched the observed glow."},{"prompt":"Which quantity of light decides whether the photoelectric effect frees any electrons?","options":["the brightness","the frequency","the color temperature of the room","the distance to the lamp"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Below a threshold frequency no electrons escape, no matter how bright the beam."},{"prompt":"A clock moving quickly past you, compared with your own clock, appears to...","options":["tick faster","tick slower","stop entirely","tick at the same rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Time dilation makes a moving clock run slow from the point of view of an observer at rest."},{"prompt":"In general relativity, the planets orbit the Sun because they...","options":["are pulled by an invisible rope","follow the straightest paths through spacetime curved by the Sun","are blown by the solar wind","repel each other magnetically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Mass curves spacetime, and free bodies follow geodesics, the straightest available paths through it."},{"prompt":"The square of the wavefunction gives the...","options":["exact position of the particle","probability of finding the particle at each place","mass of the particle","charge of the particle"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Quantum mechanics predicts odds; the squared wavefunction is the probability of finding the particle there."},{"prompt":"Which process joins light nuclei into heavier ones and powers the Sun?","options":["fission","fusion","beta decay","ionization"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fusion merges light nuclei such as hydrogen into helium, releasing energy; fission instead splits heavy nuclei."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
